    "timing": "editorial-estimate",
    "text": "In 2025, Starbucks reported over two hundred and twenty-two million dollars in revenue.\n\nFrom gift-card money customers were never expected to spend.\n\nAnd here's the surprising part.\n\nThis isn't some illegal trick. It's a recognized accounting practice used by businesses that sell gift cards.\n\nOnce you see how it works, you'll never look at a gift card the same way again.\n\nSo here's the setup.\n\nA man walks into Starbucks and buys a twenty-dollar gift card for his friend.\n\nStarbucks takes his twenty dollars immediately.\n\nBut in accounting terms, that money isn't revenue yet.\n\nIt's a promise.\n\nBecause Starbucks still owes someone twenty dollars' worth of coffee.\n\nIt's a debt wearing a gift card's clothing.\n\nNow, the friend visits Starbucks and spends fourteen dollars.\n\nSix dollars remain on the card.\n\nWeeks pass.\n\nThen months.\n\nAnd the card ends up forgotten in a drawer.\n\nNow multiply that by millions of customers.\n\nSome spend every cent.\n\nOthers leave a dollar or two behind.\n\nStarbucks studies historical redemption patterns to estimate how much of that money customers are unlikely to ever use.\n\nAnd here's where things get interesting.\n\nUnder accounting rules, Starbucks can gradually recognize a portion of those expected unused balances as revenue, in proportion to gift-card redemptions.\n\nThis has a name.\n\nGift-card breakage.\n\nAnd in fiscal 2025, Starbucks recognized over two hundred and twenty-two million dollars in breakage revenue.\n\nNot one extra latte required for those unused balances.\n\nSo here's the strange truth.\n\nA company can recognize revenue even when the customer never comes back to use the remaining gift-card balance.\n\nThe cash was collected earlier. The product may never be redeemed.\n\nSo next time you buy a gift card, ask yourself...\n\nWho's really receiving the gift?\n\nThe person you're giving it to?\n\nOr the company you bought it from?\n\nFollow Oddly Logical for more strange business secrets hiding in plain sight.",
    "sentences": [
      {
        "start": 0.45,
